1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,040
Practice.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,900
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:21,240
If your workday ever feels like a non-stop stream of emails, meetings, and urgent requests, you're definitely not alone.

6
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,390
And the good news is, you don't need complicated English to handle it.

7
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:28,870
You just need the right phrases used in the right way.

8
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,650
Welcome back to Business English Conversations.

9
00:00:32,330 --> 00:00:32,870
I'm Olivia.

10
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:34,230
And I'm Marcus.

11
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:38,540
Today's topic is real business English phrases for daily work.

12
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:46,450
We're going to go through practical workplace phrases, explain what they mean, and then show how they are used in real office conversations.

13
00:00:47,170 --> 00:00:52,140
No theory, no fluff, just real spoken English you can use immediately at work.

14
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:53,180
Let's begin.

15
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,450
First phrase, I'll keep you in the loop.

16
00:00:56,780 --> 00:01:01,830
Meaning, this means you will keep someone updated with information as things progress.

17
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:03,090
Usage.

18
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:07,580
You use it when someone wants to stay informed about a task or project.

19
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:09,440
Example conversation.

20
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,740
Any updates on the client proposal?

21
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,330
Not yet, but I'll keep you in the loop as soon as I hear anything.

22
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:16,590
Next phrase.

23
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:18,410
Let's touch base later.

24
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:22,650
This means to reconnect or talk again later about a topic.

25
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:23,540
Usage.

26
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:27,020
You use it when you want to postpone a discussion briefly.

27
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:28,920
Example conversation.

28
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:31,350
Do we need to finalize the budget now?

29
00:01:32,090 --> 00:01:32,690
Not right now.

30
00:01:32,910 --> 00:01:34,750
Let's touch base later this afternoon.

31
00:01:35,490 --> 00:01:36,060
Next phrase.

32
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:37,640
I'm on top of it.

33
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:41,520
This means you are handling a task and managing it well.

34
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:42,580
Usage.

35
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:46,710
You use it to reassure someone that a task is under control.

36
00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:48,670
Example conversation.

37
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:50,940
Did you send the updated report?

38
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:52,700
Yes, I'm on top of it.

39
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:54,170
It's already been sent to the team.

40
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:55,430
Next phrase.

41
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:57,190
Let's play it by ear.

42
00:01:57,870 --> 00:02:03,300
This means to decide things as the situation develops instead of planning everything in advance.

43
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:04,440
Usage.

44
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,540
You use it when plans are flexible or uncertain.

45
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:09,440
Example conversation.

46
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,290
Do we have a fixed schedule for the client visit?

47
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:13,770
Not exactly.

48
00:02:14,070 --> 00:02:16,530
Let's play it by ear depending on their arrival time.

49
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:17,790
Next phrase.

50
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:19,460
I'm tied up right now.

51
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:23,850
This means you are busy and cannot talk or take action at the moment.

52
00:02:24,670 --> 00:02:24,970
Usage.

53
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:27,960
You use it when you are occupied with work.

54
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:29,180
Example conversation.

55
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:31,560
Can you join the meeting in five minutes?

56
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:33,730
Sorry, I'm tied up right now.

57
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:34,950
Can we join a bit later?

58
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:36,210
Next phrase.

59
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:37,910
Let's get the ball rolling.

60
00:02:38,510 --> 00:02:40,940
This means to start a task or process.

61
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:42,080
Usage.

62
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:44,760
You use it when beginning a meeting or project.

63
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:46,640
Example conversation.

64
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,210
Are we ready to start the project discussion?

65
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:51,200
Yes, let's get the ball rolling.

66
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:54,210
Next phrase, we're short on time.

67
00:02:54,890 --> 00:02:57,330
This means there is not much time available.

68
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:01,420
Usage, you use it when you need to speed up discussion or work.

69
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:03,320
Example conversation.

70
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,140
Should we go through every slide in detail?

71
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,180
No, we're short on time.

72
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:10,030
Let's focus on key points only.

73
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:13,890
Next phrase, that's not my area of expertise.

74
00:03:14,570 --> 00:03:18,640
This means the topic is outside your knowledge or responsibility.

75
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:19,350
Usage.

76
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:22,330
You use it to redirect questions professionally.

77
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:24,290
Example conversation.

78
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,140
Can you explain the technical server issue?

79
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:31,440
That's not my area of expertise, but I can connect you with IT.

80
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:32,740
Next phrase.

81
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,450
Let's take a closer look.

82
00:03:35,170 --> 00:03:37,810
This means to examine something in more detail.

83
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:38,950
Usage.

84
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:42,530
You use it when analyzing data, reports, or problems.

85
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:44,560
Example conversation.

86
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:46,700
Sales dropped last week.

87
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:50,350
Let's take a closer look at the numbers before making conclusions.

88
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:53,620
Next phrase, I'll run it by my manager.

89
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:57,680
This means you will ask your manager for approval or opinion.

90
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:01,930
Usage, you use it when you cannot make a final decision alone.

91
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:03,830
Example conversation.

92
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:06,460
Can we approve this discount for the client?

93
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,200
I'll run it by my manager and get back to you.

94
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:12,260
Next phrase, let's stay on track.

95
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:17,090
This means to continue working according to the plan without delays.

96
00:04:17,510 --> 00:04:17,970
Usage.

97
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:21,850
You use it during meetings or projects to maintain focus.

98
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:23,820
Example conversation.

99
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:27,300
We still need to discuss marketing and finance updates.

100
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,460
Yes, let's stay on track and finish on time.

101
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:31,810
Next phrase.

102
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:33,130
I'll look into it.

103
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:36,530
This means you will investigate or check a problem.

104
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:37,610
Usage.

105
00:04:37,870 --> 00:04:39,910
You use it when someone reports an issue.

106
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:41,830
Example conversation.

107
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:44,200
The system is not loading properly.

108
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:46,740
I'll look into it and update you shortly.

109
00:04:47,290 --> 00:04:49,240
Next phrase, let's wrap it up.

110
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,220
This means to finish a meeting or discussion.

111
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:55,560
Usage, you use it when all topics have been covered.

112
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,430
Example conversation.

113
00:04:58,130 --> 00:05:00,070
We've covered all agenda points.

114
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:02,890
Great, let's wrap it up and send the summary email.

115
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:05,820
Next phrase, we're aligned on this.

116
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:09,920
This means everyone agrees and understands the same plan.

117
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:13,620
Usage, you use it to confirm agreement in discussions.

118
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:15,650
Example conversation.

119
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:19,160
so we will launch next Friday with the updated design.

120
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:21,200
Yes, we're aligned on this.

121
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:25,450
Next phrase, let's prioritize this task.

122
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:28,470
This means to focus on this task before others.

123
00:05:29,370 --> 00:05:32,440
Usage, you use it when managing multiple responsibilities.

124
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:34,340
Example conversation.

125
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,660
We have three pending reports.

126
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,400
Let's prioritize this task first since it's urgent.

127
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:43,630
Next phrase, I appreciate your support.

128
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:47,190
This means you are thankful for help or cooperation.

129
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:48,360
Usage.

130
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:51,080
You use it in polite professional communication.

131
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,980
Example conversation.

132
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:55,950
I'll help you finish the report before the deadline.

133
00:05:56,590 --> 00:05:58,210
I really appreciate your support.

134
00:05:58,930 --> 00:05:59,510
Next phrase.

135
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:01,510
Let's double-check everything.

136
00:06:02,210 --> 00:06:05,860
This means to review all details carefully before finalizing.

137
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:06,940
Usage.

138
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:09,310
You use it when accuracy is important.

139
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:11,170
Example conversation.

140
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:13,410
Can we send the proposal now?

141
00:06:13,780 --> 00:06:16,900
Not yet, let's double check everything first.

142
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,020
Next phrase, we'll take it from here.

143
00:06:19,710 --> 00:06:23,510
This means your team will continue handling the task.

144
00:06:23,530 --> 00:06:27,100
Usage, you use it when taking responsibility for work.

145
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:28,280
Example conversation.

146
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:31,100
I've completed the initial setup.

147
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:32,340
Great, we'll take it from here.

148
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:35,570
Final phrase, let's keep things simple.

149
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:38,970
This means to avoid unnecessary complexity.

150
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:43,180
Usage, you use it when planning or explaining processes.

151
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:44,880
Example conversation.

152
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:47,640
Should we add more steps to the workflow?

153
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:50,950
No, let's keep things simple for better efficiency.

154
00:06:50,990 --> 00:06:55,500
That brings us to the end of today's business English phrases for daily work.

155
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:02,100
These phrases are commonly used in real offices, meetings, emails, and teamwork situations.

156
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:06,650
Practice them regularly and try using them in your daily conversations at work.

157
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:11,180
The more naturally you use them, the more fluent and confident you will become.

158
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:12,880
Thanks for listening, everyone.

159
00:07:13,330 --> 00:07:16,030
and we'll see you in the next Business English Conversation.

160
00:07:16,750 --> 00:07:17,940
Take care and goodbye.

161
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:21,860
Welcome to today's Professional English Training Podcast.

162
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:23,400
We're delighted to have you with us.

163
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:34,200
Whether you work in an office, attend meetings regularly, communicate with clients, or simply want to sound more natural in business situations, today's episode is designed for you.

164
00:07:34,260 --> 00:07:35,200
I'm Michael.

165
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:36,220
And I'm Emma.

166
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:41,250
In today's session, we'll focus on real business English phrases for daily work.

167
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:47,500
We won't just teach vocabulary, we'll show you how professionals actually speak in modern workplaces.

168
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:58,420
You'll learn useful phrases, their meanings, and practical example conversations you can use immediately in meetings, emails, presentations, teamwork, and office discussions.

169
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:04,440
The goal is simple, helping you communicate clearly, confidently, and professionally in English.

170
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:07,370
So, let's begin with our first business phrase.

171
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:10,050
The phrase is, I'll take care of it.

172
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:13,360
Meaning, I will handle the task or solve the problem.

173
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:17,340
Professionals use this phrase to show responsibility and confidence.

174
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:19,410
Practical example conversation.

175
00:08:19,790 --> 00:08:22,750
The client still hasn't received the updated contract.

176
00:08:23,190 --> 00:08:23,670
Don't worry.

177
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:25,060
I'll take care of it right away.

178
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:25,720
Thanks.

179
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,520
We need to send it before noon.

180
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:28,500
Understood.

181
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:29,940
I'll handle it immediately.

182
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:30,740
Excellent.

183
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:32,910
Here's another useful business phrase.

184
00:08:33,330 --> 00:08:34,470
Let's stay on track.

185
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:38,860
Meaning, let's continue following the plan and avoid delays.

186
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:42,170
This phrase is common during meetings and project discussions.

187
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:44,190
Practical example conversation.

188
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:47,270
We only have two days left before the presentation.

189
00:08:47,670 --> 00:08:48,050
I know.

190
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:50,960
Let's stay on track and finish the final slides today.

191
00:08:51,010 --> 00:08:51,780
Agreed.

192
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:54,000
We can review everything tomorrow morning.

193
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:54,740
Perfect.

194
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:57,760
Our next phrase is, can you walk me through it?

195
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:01,190
Meaning, can you explain the process step by step?

196
00:09:01,630 --> 00:09:05,810
This expression is very common in offices when learning new systems or tasks.

197
00:09:06,270 --> 00:09:07,590
Example conversation.

198
00:09:07,670 --> 00:09:10,520
I'm still confused about the new reporting software.

199
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:11,460
No problem.

200
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:13,300
I can walk you through it after lunch.

201
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:14,960
That would be really helpful.

202
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,710
It's actually much easier than it looks.

203
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:20,990
Another important phrase is, we're on the same page.

204
00:09:21,450 --> 00:09:24,390
Meaning, we understand each other clearly and agree.

205
00:09:24,870 --> 00:09:25,950
Example conversation.

206
00:09:26,410 --> 00:09:32,040
So the marketing team will handle the campaign and the sales team will contact the clients directly.

207
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:32,860
Correct.

208
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:36,800
Just making sure we're all on the same page before moving forward.

209
00:09:36,990 --> 00:09:37,670
Absolutely.

210
00:09:37,990 --> 00:09:39,090
Everything sounds clear.

211
00:09:39,490 --> 00:09:39,750
Great.

212
00:09:40,010 --> 00:09:41,650
Here's another professional phrase.

213
00:09:42,190 --> 00:09:43,050
I'll get back to you.

214
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:46,400
Meaning, I'll give you an answer or update later.

215
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:49,420
Very useful when you don't have immediate information.

216
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:51,040
Example conversation.

217
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:53,210
Do we know the final budget yet?

218
00:09:53,630 --> 00:09:54,170
Not yet.

219
00:09:54,510 --> 00:09:56,390
I still need approval from finance.

220
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:58,230
I'll get back to you this afternoon.

221
00:09:58,690 --> 00:09:59,170
Sounds good.

222
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:02,220
Next phrase, let's keep the momentum going.

223
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:05,660
Meaning, let's continue making good progress.

224
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:07,080
Example conversation.

225
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,190
Sales increased by 18% this month.

226
00:10:10,550 --> 00:10:11,850
That's fantastic news.

227
00:10:12,250 --> 00:10:14,110
Let's keep the momentum going next quarter.

228
00:10:14,650 --> 00:10:15,230
Absolutely.

229
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:17,180
The team has been working really hard.

230
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:18,900
It's definitely paying off.

231
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:20,780
Here's another useful expression.

232
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:22,710
I'd like to highlight something.

233
00:10:23,050 --> 00:10:25,990
Meaning, I want to emphasize an important point.

234
00:10:26,470 --> 00:10:27,730
Example conversation.

235
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:30,890
Before we finish the meeting, I'd like to highlight something.

236
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:32,130
Sure, go ahead.

237
00:10:32,490 --> 00:10:36,890
Customer satisfaction improved significantly after the new support system launched.

238
00:10:37,310 --> 00:10:38,550
That's excellent progress.

239
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,040
Our next business phrase is, we may need to rethink our strategy.

240
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:45,980
Meaning, we may need to change our plan.

241
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:47,490
Example conversation.

242
00:10:47,890 --> 00:10:50,190
The campaign results are lower than expected.

243
00:10:50,630 --> 00:10:51,750
I noticed that too.

244
00:10:52,110 --> 00:10:53,970
We may need to rethink our strategy.

245
00:10:54,470 --> 00:10:57,520
Maybe we should focus more on social media advertising.

246
00:10:57,630 --> 00:10:59,340
That could definitely help.

247
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:01,440
Here's another important workplace phrase.

248
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,200
Let's circle back later.

249
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:06,020
Meaning, let's return to this topic later.

250
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:09,390
This phrase is extremely common in professional meetings.

251
00:11:09,850 --> 00:11:10,930
Example conversation.

252
00:11:11,650 --> 00:11:14,530
We still don't have enough information to make a decision.

253
00:11:14,570 --> 00:11:18,360
In that case, let's circle back later after we review the data.

254
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:19,180
Good idea.

255
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,340
We should have clearer numbers tomorrow.

256
00:11:21,830 --> 00:11:25,090
Another excellent phrase is, I appreciate you bringing this up.

257
00:11:25,470 --> 00:11:27,890
Meaning, thank you for mentioning this issue.

258
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:29,430
Example conversation.

259
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:32,700
Some employees are struggling with the new schedule.

260
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:34,560
I appreciate you bringing this up.

261
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:36,620
We should discuss possible solutions.

262
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:37,490
I agree.

263
00:11:37,550 --> 00:11:39,270
Employee feedback is important.

264
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:40,250
Absolutely.

265
00:11:40,290 --> 00:11:42,950
Let's continue with another business phrase.

266
00:11:43,490 --> 00:11:44,720
What's the status update?

267
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,300
Meaning, what's the current progress?

268
00:11:47,410 --> 00:11:48,640
Example conversation.

269
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:51,720
What's the status update on the website redesign?

270
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:54,980
The development team completed most of the work yesterday.

271
00:11:55,380 --> 00:11:55,800
Excellent.

272
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:57,390
Are we still on schedule?

273
00:11:57,790 --> 00:11:58,090
Yes.

274
00:11:58,390 --> 00:11:59,730
Everything looks good so far.

275
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:02,210
Here's another phrase professionals use daily.

276
00:12:02,750 --> 00:12:04,320
I'd recommend moving forward.

277
00:12:04,650 --> 00:12:07,220
Meaning, I suggest continuing with the plan.

278
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:08,740
Example conversation.

279
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:11,060
Do you think we should approve the proposal?

280
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:11,860
Yes.

281
00:12:12,250 --> 00:12:15,010
After reviewing the numbers, I'd recommend moving forward.

282
00:12:15,170 --> 00:12:15,530
Great.

283
00:12:15,870 --> 00:12:17,240
I'll notify the client today.

284
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:17,980
Perfect.

285
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:21,520
Another useful phrase is, let's prioritize this.

286
00:12:22,020 --> 00:12:25,230
Meaning, this task is important and should be done first.

287
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:26,730
Example conversation.

288
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:30,230
We received a complaint from one of our biggest clients.

289
00:12:30,770 --> 00:12:33,480
In that case, let's prioritize this immediately.

290
00:12:33,890 --> 00:12:34,260
Agreed.

291
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:37,080
Customer relationships are extremely important.

292
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:38,000
Definitely.

293
00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:39,920
Here's another professional expression.

294
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:42,020
That's outside my area.

295
00:12:42,140 --> 00:12:45,640
Meaning, that's not my responsibility or expertise.

296
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:47,210
Example conversation.

297
00:12:47,870 --> 00:12:50,030
Can you help me with the legal contract review?

298
00:12:50,350 --> 00:12:52,470
Honestly, that's outside my area.

299
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:55,010
You should probably speak with the legal department.

300
00:12:55,510 --> 00:12:56,000
Good point.

301
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:57,400
I'll contact them directly.

302
00:12:57,780 --> 00:13:00,840
Another excellent phrase is, let's aim for Friday.

303
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:03,740
Meaning, let's try to finish by Friday.

304
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:05,310
Example conversation.

305
00:13:05,790 --> 00:13:07,390
When should we complete the report?

306
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:11,670
Let's aim for Friday so we have time to review everything before submission.

307
00:13:12,170 --> 00:13:12,850
Sounds reasonable.

308
00:13:13,250 --> 00:13:14,170
I'll inform the team.

309
00:13:14,650 --> 00:13:16,420
Here's another natural business phrase.

310
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:18,260
I just wanted to follow up.

311
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:22,350
Meaning, I'm checking for updates or reminding you about something.

312
00:13:22,830 --> 00:13:23,890
Example conversation.

313
00:13:24,690 --> 00:13:25,190
Hi, Daniel.

314
00:13:25,470 --> 00:13:28,210
I just wanted to follow up regarding the client presentation.

315
00:13:28,590 --> 00:13:29,630
Thanks for reminding me.

316
00:13:29,650 --> 00:13:31,780
I'll send the updated version shortly.

317
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:32,540
Perfect.

318
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:34,420
The client meeting is tomorrow morning.

319
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:35,120
Understood.

320
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:39,200
Another common office phrase is, we need a quick turnaround.

321
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,140
Meaning, we need this completed very quickly.

322
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:43,620
Example conversation.

323
00:13:44,330 --> 00:13:46,750
The client changed the design requirements again.

324
00:13:46,810 --> 00:13:50,910
All right, but we need a quick turnaround because the deadline is tomorrow.

325
00:13:51,370 --> 00:13:53,610
I'll coordinate with the design team immediately.

326
00:13:53,650 --> 00:13:54,530
Excellent.

327
00:13:54,880 --> 00:13:56,440
Here's a practical business phrase.

328
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:58,080
Thanks for your flexibility.

329
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:02,280
Meaning, thank you for adjusting or adapting to changes.

330
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:03,580
Example conversation.

331
00:14:04,070 --> 00:14:06,210
I know the schedule changed at the last minute.

332
00:14:06,630 --> 00:14:07,150
No worries.

333
00:14:07,590 --> 00:14:08,870
Thanks for your flexibility.

334
00:14:09,090 --> 00:14:10,210
It really helped the team.

335
00:14:10,670 --> 00:14:11,270
Happy to help.

336
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:15,080
Another powerful phrase is, we should think long-term.

337
00:14:15,580 --> 00:14:19,960
Meaning, we should consider future results, not just immediate results.

338
00:14:19,980 --> 00:14:21,040
Example conversation.

339
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:23,750
This solution is cheaper right now.

340
00:14:24,270 --> 00:14:28,030
True, but we should think long-term before making the final decision.

341
00:14:28,150 --> 00:14:28,870
Good point.

342
00:14:29,170 --> 00:14:30,970
Long-term stability matters more.

343
00:14:31,470 --> 00:14:32,010
Exactly.

344
00:14:32,470 --> 00:14:33,890
One final phrase for today.

345
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:35,560
Let's make this happen.

346
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:38,800
Meaning, let's successfully achieve this goal.

347
00:14:39,260 --> 00:14:40,340
Example conversation.

348
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:43,920
The client officially approved the new project this morning.

349
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:45,370
Fantastic news!

350
00:14:45,770 --> 00:14:46,750
Let's make this happen.

351
00:14:47,150 --> 00:14:48,790
The whole team is excited to begin.

352
00:14:49,250 --> 00:14:51,790
This is going to be a great opportunity for the company.

353
00:14:52,230 --> 00:14:56,740
As you can hear, real business English is often simple, clear, and direct.

354
00:14:56,900 --> 00:15:00,170
You don't need complicated vocabulary to sound professional.

355
00:15:00,590 --> 00:15:04,350
What matters most is confidence, clarity, and regular practice.

356
00:15:04,710 --> 00:15:09,700
Try using these phrases during meetings, emails, calls, and workplace conversations.

357
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:13,500
The more naturally you use them, the more fluent you'll sound.

358
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:15,520
Thank you for joining today's session.

359
00:15:15,860 --> 00:15:20,610
We hope these practical business English phrases help you communicate more professionally at work.

360
00:15:21,010 --> 00:15:22,470
Keep practicing every day.

361
00:15:22,870 --> 00:15:24,970
And we'll see you again in the next episode.

362
00:15:25,350 --> 00:15:26,770
