369
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:50,130
Whether you're working in an office, attending meetings online, or preparing for an international job, today's lesson is going to be incredibly useful.

370
00:15:50,630 --> 00:15:55,720
That's because today we're talking about real business English phrases for daily work.

371
00:15:55,970 --> 00:16:01,750
We're going to teach you practical phrases that people actually use every day in real workplaces.

372
00:16:02,370 --> 00:16:09,040
And not only that, we'll explain the meaning of each phrase and show you practical examples through natural office conversations.

373
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:16,330
So instead of memorizing difficult grammar rules, you'll learn how real professionals communicate naturally at work.

374
00:16:16,890 --> 00:16:18,430
All right, Mia, let's jump right in.

375
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:19,500
Let's do it.

376
00:16:19,980 --> 00:16:22,780
Okay, everyone, here's today's first business phrase.

377
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:24,620
I'm tied up right now.

378
00:16:24,910 --> 00:16:27,950
This phrase means you're very busy at the moment.

379
00:16:27,970 --> 00:16:31,520
Native English speakers use this expression constantly at work.

380
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:36,820
Instead of always saying, I'm busy, this sounds much more natural and fluent.

381
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:37,960
Example conversation.

382
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,630
Hey, Mia, can you help me with this report right now?

383
00:16:41,190 --> 00:16:44,390
I'm sorry, I'm tied up right now, but I can help you after lunch.

384
00:16:45,090 --> 00:16:46,780
Very practical office English.

385
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:47,940
Definitely.

386
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:49,640
Here's another common phrase.

387
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:51,940
Let's keep things on track.

388
00:16:52,230 --> 00:16:55,650
The meaning is, let's continue making progress without delays.

389
00:16:56,290 --> 00:17:00,260
Managers use this expression all the time during projects and meetings.

390
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:01,960
Example conversation.

391
00:17:02,540 --> 00:17:05,220
We lost two days because of technical problems.

392
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:08,710
I know, so let's keep things on track from now on.

393
00:17:09,390 --> 00:17:11,150
Excellent professional communication.

394
00:17:11,710 --> 00:17:13,330
Here's another useful business phrase.

395
00:17:13,970 --> 00:17:14,440
I'm on it.

396
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:17,000
This means I'm already working on the task.

397
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:19,680
Very short, confident, and natural.

398
00:17:20,260 --> 00:17:21,530
Example conversation.

399
00:17:21,830 --> 00:17:24,490
Did you send the updated proposal to the client?

400
00:17:25,090 --> 00:17:26,150
Not yet, but I'm on it.

401
00:17:26,750 --> 00:17:31,280
Native speakers love using this phrase because it sounds efficient and professional.

402
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:32,520
Absolutely.

403
00:17:33,040 --> 00:17:35,480
Here's another phrase you'll hear daily in offices.

404
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:37,270
Let's circle back later.

405
00:17:37,810 --> 00:17:40,350
This means, let's discuss this again later.

406
00:17:41,070 --> 00:17:44,440
Very common in meetings when people need more time or information.

407
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:46,080
Example conversation.

408
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:49,180
We still don't have enough data to make a decision.

409
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:51,930
All right, let's circle back later this afternoon.

410
00:17:52,670 --> 00:17:54,230
Very natural business English.

411
00:17:54,790 --> 00:17:55,930
Here's another important one.

412
00:17:56,470 --> 00:17:57,370
He'll walk me through it.

413
00:17:57,950 --> 00:18:00,930
Meaning, I'll explain the process step by step.

414
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:05,420
Managers and coworkers use this expression constantly while training employees.

415
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:07,100
Example conversation.

416
00:18:07,700 --> 00:18:09,420
I've never used this software before.

417
00:18:10,050 --> 00:18:11,590
No worries, I'll walk you through it.

418
00:18:12,170 --> 00:18:14,190
Friendly and professional at the same time.

419
00:18:14,750 --> 00:18:17,290
Here's another phrase fluent speakers use often.

420
00:18:17,890 --> 00:18:19,470
We need to sort this out.

421
00:18:19,610 --> 00:18:21,930
This means we need to solve the problem.

422
00:18:22,550 --> 00:18:23,590
Example conversation.

423
00:18:25,190 --> 00:18:28,300
That's serious.

424
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:30,460
We need to sort this out immediately.

425
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:32,300
Great workplace phrase.

426
00:18:32,870 --> 00:18:34,790
Here's another practical business expression.

427
00:18:35,490 --> 00:18:39,210
Meaning you have too much work to do.

428
00:18:39,820 --> 00:18:40,940
Example conversation.

429
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:46,420
Honestly, I'm already swamped with work.

430
00:18:47,070 --> 00:18:48,570
Very common office English.

431
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:50,310
Here's another phrase you should know.

432
00:18:50,910 --> 00:18:52,530
That's not a priority right now.

433
00:18:53,130 --> 00:18:56,090
This means the task is not very important at the moment.

434
00:18:56,710 --> 00:18:57,760
Example conversation.

435
00:18:58,340 --> 00:19:00,820
Should we redesign the office website today?

436
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:01,860
Not yet.

437
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:03,420
That's not a priority right now.

438
00:19:04,020 --> 00:19:06,040
Clear and professional communication.

439
00:19:06,610 --> 00:19:07,950
Here's another natural phrase.

440
00:19:08,490 --> 00:19:09,510
We're on the right track.

441
00:19:10,110 --> 00:19:12,970
Meaning things are progressing correctly or successfully.

442
00:19:13,550 --> 00:19:14,630
Example conversation.

443
00:19:15,210 --> 00:19:17,780
Customer feedback has improved a lot this month.

444
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:20,600
Great, that means we're on the right track.

445
00:19:20,620 --> 00:19:23,170
Positive business language is very important.

446
00:19:23,730 --> 00:19:24,270
Definitely.

447
00:19:24,850 --> 00:19:26,250
Here's another useful expression.

448
00:19:26,790 --> 00:19:27,670
Can you handle this?

449
00:19:28,350 --> 00:19:30,940
Meaning, can you manage this task or situation?

450
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:32,580
Example conversation.

451
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:35,580
I need someone to speak with the supplier this afternoon.

452
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,460
Sure, I can handle this.

453
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:39,590
Confident and professional.

454
00:19:40,070 --> 00:19:42,490
Here's another phrase professionals use every day.

455
00:19:42,550 --> 00:19:43,970
I'll make it happen.

456
00:19:44,590 --> 00:19:47,600
Meaning, you promise to complete something successfully.

457
00:19:47,960 --> 00:19:49,140
Example conversation.

458
00:19:49,740 --> 00:19:52,590
We need the presentation finished before tomorrow morning.

459
00:19:53,250 --> 00:19:53,650
Understood.

460
00:19:53,930 --> 00:19:54,630
I'll make it happen.

461
00:19:55,270 --> 00:19:56,950
Strong business English right there.

462
00:19:57,570 --> 00:19:58,890
Here's another excellent phrase.

463
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:01,080
Let's think outside the box.

464
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:03,580
This means let's think creatively.

465
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:05,200
Example conversation.

466
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:07,760
Sales numbers are dropping again.

467
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:11,750
Then let's think outside the box and try new marketing ideas.

468
00:20:12,450 --> 00:20:15,130
Very popular expression in business meetings.

469
00:20:15,150 --> 00:20:16,650
Here's another important one.

470
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:17,920
I need a quick update.

471
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:21,610
Meaning you want brief new information about progress.

472
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:23,310
Example conversation.

473
00:20:23,970 --> 00:20:27,260
Before the meeting starts, I need a quick update on the project status.

474
00:20:27,920 --> 00:20:29,960
Very realistic office communication.

475
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:32,160
Here's another phrase you'll hear often.

476
00:20:32,750 --> 00:20:33,910
We're running out of time.

477
00:20:33,970 --> 00:20:36,730
This means there's very little time left.

478
00:20:37,370 --> 00:20:38,410
Example conversation.

479
00:20:39,060 --> 00:20:41,400
The client presentation starts in 20 minutes.

480
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:44,360
Then let's finish these slides quickly.

481
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:45,870
We're running out of time.

482
00:20:46,150 --> 00:20:47,610
Excellent workplace English.

483
00:20:48,170 --> 00:20:49,950
Here's another practical business phrase.

484
00:20:50,630 --> 00:20:51,580
I'll take it from here.

485
00:20:52,260 --> 00:20:54,640
Meaning, I'll continue handling the situation.

486
00:20:55,240 --> 00:20:56,320
Example conversation.

487
00:20:56,960 --> 00:20:59,510
The customer still has concerns about the contract.

488
00:21:00,090 --> 00:21:03,070
All right, I'll take it from here and speak with them personally.

489
00:21:03,790 --> 00:21:06,200
Very professional leadership language.

490
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:07,520
Here's another useful phrase.

491
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:08,840
We're getting there.

492
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:12,000
Meaning, progress is happening even if slowly.

493
00:21:12,590 --> 00:21:13,830
Example conversation.

494
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:16,260
The software testing still isn't complete.

495
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:18,250
I know, but we're getting there.

496
00:21:18,890 --> 00:21:20,230
Encouraging and positive.

497
00:21:20,830 --> 00:21:22,570
Here's another natural office phrase.

498
00:21:23,190 --> 00:21:24,920
Let's not jump to conclusions.

499
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:28,940
This means let's not decide too quickly without enough information.

500
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:30,630
Example conversation.

501
00:21:31,250 --> 00:21:32,890
The client hasn't replied all day.

502
00:21:33,370 --> 00:21:34,850
Maybe they rejected the proposal.

503
00:21:35,510 --> 00:21:37,440
Let's not jump to conclusions yet.

504
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:41,920
Here's another important one.

505
00:21:42,190 --> 00:21:44,050
We need to streamline the process.

506
00:21:44,690 --> 00:21:48,580
Meaning, make the process faster, simpler, or more efficient.

507
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:50,260
Example conversation.

508
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:53,580
Employees spend too much time filling out paperwork.

509
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:54,640
Agreed.

510
00:21:54,890 --> 00:21:56,630
We need to streamline the process.

511
00:21:57,370 --> 00:21:59,050
Excellent management vocabulary.

512
00:21:59,610 --> 00:22:02,100
Here's another fluent professionals use naturally.

513
00:22:02,690 --> 00:22:03,720
I'll keep an eye on it.

514
00:22:04,260 --> 00:22:06,700
Meaning, I'll monitor the situation carefully.

515
00:22:07,320 --> 00:22:08,380
Example conversation.

516
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,290
The server had problems earlier this morning.

517
00:22:11,670 --> 00:22:14,090
Don't worry, I'll keep an eye on it throughout the day.

518
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:16,420
Very practical business English.

519
00:22:16,980 --> 00:22:18,940
Here's another strong workplace phrase.

520
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:21,180
We need to step up our game.

521
00:22:21,870 --> 00:22:24,510
Meaning improve performance or work harder.

522
00:22:25,170 --> 00:22:26,230
Example conversation.

523
00:22:26,830 --> 00:22:29,340
Our competitors are growing much faster than us.

524
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:31,660
Then we need to step up our game.

525
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:34,080
Motivational business language.

526
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:36,050
Here's another excellent phrase.

527
00:22:36,730 --> 00:22:38,490
I don't want to overpromise.

528
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,600
This means you don't want to promise something unrealistic.

529
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:43,300
Example conversation.

530
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:46,090
Can your team finish the project in two days?

531
00:22:46,710 --> 00:22:48,950
Honestly, I don't want to over-promise.

532
00:22:49,170 --> 00:22:50,470
We may need more time.

533
00:22:51,150 --> 00:22:53,530
Very professional and honest communication.

534
00:22:54,070 --> 00:22:56,200
Here's another phrase you'll hear often in meetings.

535
00:22:56,860 --> 00:22:58,300
Let's get everyone aligned.

536
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:02,180
Meaning, make sure everyone agrees and understands the plan.

537
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:03,900
Example conversation.

538
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:07,550
Different departments seem confused about the new strategy.

539
00:23:07,870 --> 00:23:10,870
Then let's get everyone aligned before moving forward.

540
00:23:11,550 --> 00:23:12,890
Great teamwork expression.

541
00:23:13,430 --> 00:23:15,150
Here's another practical business phrase.

542
00:23:15,790 --> 00:23:16,950
That's a solid idea.

543
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:19,880
Meaning the idea is strong and reliable.

544
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:21,560
Example conversation.

545
00:23:22,220 --> 00:23:24,660
What if we offer customers a free trial period?

546
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:26,330
That's a solid idea.

547
00:23:26,390 --> 00:23:27,690
It could increase sales.

548
00:23:28,370 --> 00:23:29,890
Very natural meeting English.

549
00:23:30,470 --> 00:23:31,650
Here's another useful one.

550
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:33,590
We need to act fast.

551
00:23:34,190 --> 00:23:36,760
Meaning immediate action is necessary.

552
00:23:37,090 --> 00:23:38,250
Example conversation.

553
00:23:38,830 --> 00:23:41,520
The competitor launched a similar product this morning.

554
00:23:42,140 --> 00:23:43,720
Then we need to act fast.

555
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:46,400
Short, powerful, and professional.

556
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:49,890
Here's another phrase fluent professionals use all the time.

557
00:23:50,510 --> 00:23:51,950
Let's touch base tomorrow.

558
00:23:52,550 --> 00:23:54,610
Meaning, let's communicate again tomorrow.

559
00:23:55,170 --> 00:23:56,210
Example conversation.

560
00:23:56,840 --> 00:23:58,740
I still need approval from upper management.

561
00:23:59,320 --> 00:23:59,800
Understood.

562
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:01,860
Let's touch base tomorrow afternoon.

563
00:24:02,540 --> 00:24:04,440
Extremely common in workplaces.

564
00:24:05,140 --> 00:24:06,310
Absolutely.

565
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:09,570
